(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-study-design-research-methods-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Study Design and Research Methods Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is a prospective cohort study?","choices":["The investigator goes back in history to define a risk group","Groups are defined by outcome and examined for past exposure","Groups are assembled now by exposure and followed forward","Exposure is assigned at random by the investigator"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with baseline data collected at the outset. It is also called a concurrent cohort study, because observation runs alongside the events. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does a good design allow beyond comparing groups?","choices":["Eliminating chance entirely from the results","Guaranteeing the results will generalize to any population","Quantifying the risk difference and determining temporal sequence","Removing the need for statistical analysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and temporal sequence is the one that bears on causation: knowing which came first is necessary before an exposure can be held responsible for an outcome. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why must follow-up of a cohort be complete?","choices":["So the sample size remains statistically significant","So the groups can be randomly reassigned","So the exposure can be measured again at the end","So attrition does not falsely skew the outcome data"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the period must also be long enough for the expected outcome to appear, since a cohort followed too briefly will show nothing whatever the truth. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the purpose of a survey as a research method?","choices":["To assign participants to treatment groups","To get an overview of the research question","To follow a defined group forward over years","To pool results across several published studies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, by developing and administering questions built for that purpose. It is classed as observational, since nothing is assigned. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the typical sequence of study types investigating a question?","choices":["Observations, available data, case-control, cohort, trials","Randomized trials, cohort, case-control, clinical observations","Meta-analysis, systematic review, cohort, case-control","Case-control, randomized trials, cohort, clinical observations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the order reflects cost and certainty together: cheap observations generate the hypothesis that an expensive trial is eventually built to test. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What does longitudinal research measure?","choices":["A single moment in a population","Changes over time","The effect of an assigned intervention","Whether a program or policy is working"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is observational: the investigator records what changes rather than producing the change. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"When is data obtained in a cross-sectional survey?","choices":["At two points separated by several years","At a single point in time","Continuously throughout the study period","Retrospectively, from historical records"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with risk factor and disease measured together, and the analysis performed on individual level data rather than population aggregates. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What kind of data does quantitative research use?","choices":["Observation, field notes and audio recordings","Existing data collected by other researchers","Numerical, objective and measurable","Narrative accounts of individual experience"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the paradigm is positivistic and deductive, aimed at developing predictions rather than the descriptions qualitative work produces. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is policy research or evaluation used to determine?","choices":["Whether a program works or a policy is helpful","Whether an experimental treatment is effective","How a characteristic is distributed at one point in time","How outcomes change across many years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is classed as observational, since the program or policy was put in place for its own reasons rather than assigned by the researcher. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What distinguishes quasi-experimental from experimental research?","choices":["The researcher controls the assignment but not the treatment","The researcher controls the treatment but not the assignment","The researcher controls neither treatment nor assignment","The researcher controls both, but the study is smaller"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and that is why it has less control than a true experiment: without control of assignment, the groups may differ in ways the treatment did not create. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which designs are given as examples of descriptive work?","choices":["Cohort and case-control studies","Randomized controlled trials","Case report and case series","Systematic reviews and meta-analyses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and both sit low in the evidence hierarchy precisely because describing a group supports no comparison and therefore no inference about cause. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the essential difference between a cohort and a case-control study?","choices":["Cohort groups by exposure, case-control groups by outcome","Cohort is experimental, case-control is observational","Cohort measures at one moment, case-control over time","Cohort is secondary research, case-control is primary"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the direction of enquiry follows: the cohort looks forward from exposure to outcome, the case-control backward from outcome to exposure. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What distinguishes a cross-sectional ecologic study?","choices":["It follows individuals forward over many years","It assigns an exposure to half the population","It measures each participant twice","It uses population rather than individual level data"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, relating the frequency of a characteristic to an outcome of interest across populations rather than within individuals. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What kinds of blinding are possible in a randomized controlled trial?","choices":["Blinding of the statistician only","No blinding is possible in a randomized trial","Blinding is required in every randomized trial","Single or double blinding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and blinding matters because it protects against performance bias: participants who know they are on placebo may seek other treatment. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What kind of design is a randomized controlled trial?","choices":["Observational, for testing hypotheses","Experimental, for testing hypotheses","Observational, for generating hypotheses","Descriptive, for characterizing a single group"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because the investigator assigns and manipulates the intervention rather than observing an exposure that arose on its own. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is the purpose of an analytic design?","choices":["To describe one group as fully as possible","To compare groups and draw an inference for a population","To pool the results of several published studies","To generate a hypothesis without testing it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the inference is the point: comparison is what allows a finding in the study to say something about people who were not in it. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is observed rather than assigned in an observational design?","choices":["The outcome, which is the independent variable","The covariate, which distorts the relationship","The exposure, which is the independent variable","The sample, which is drawn from the population"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and naming it the independent variable is the link back to the basic vocabulary: it is the possible cause, whether the investigator allocated it or not. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where does a cross-sectional study fall on the two axes?","choices":["It is analytic and experimental","It is descriptive only","It is experimental only","Under both descriptive and analytic, and observational"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that dual placement reflects its range: it can simply describe a population at one moment, or relate a characteristic to an outcome. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which instruments are used in primary research?","choices":["Meta-analyses and systematic reviews","Published guidelines and expert consensus statements","Disease registries maintained by state governments","Questionnaires, surveys, measurements and lab results"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and what they have in common is that the investigator generates the data rather than drawing on data someone else has already collected. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is a cross-sectional design for?","choices":["Generating hypotheses","Testing hypotheses experimentally","Following a cohort forward to an outcome","Pooling data from published studies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is observational, and because risk factor and disease are measured at the same moment, it cannot establish which came first. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What do weaker designs establish, if not cause and effect?","choices":["Relationships or predictability","Nothing that can be used clinically","Only the prevalence of a condition","The cost-effectiveness of an intervention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction is practical: knowing that two things travel together is useful even when it does not show that one produces the other. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which is an example of a cross-sectional survey?","choices":["Following smokers for twenty years to observe lung cancer","Varicella titres showing what proportion of a population is immune","Comparing people with lung cancer to people without for past smoking","Assigning patients to a vaccine or a placebo"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside determining obesity in a population at a point in time and colorectal cancer screening by fecal occult blood testing. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How are groups defined in a case-control study?","choices":["By exposure, as exposed or unexposed","By random assignment to treatment or control","By outcome, as having the disease or not","By the point in time at which they were measured"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and they are then assessed for a difference in exposure or risk factors, which means the study looks backward from outcome to cause. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Why must a randomized trial account for withdrawals and drop-outs?","choices":["Drop-outs must be replaced to preserve the sample size","Drop-outs invalidate random assignment","Losing participants can distort the outcome","Drop-outs prevent blinding from being maintained"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is the same concern that requires complete follow-up of a cohort: participants who leave may differ from those who stay. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is study design?","choices":["The organization and plan for data collection and analysis","The statistical tests applied once data exist","The number of participants enrolled","The clinical question the study sets out to answer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it matters because it bears directly on the validity and reliability of the data: strong design gives strong data, and strong data gives valid conclusions. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is said about the relative strength of study designs?","choices":["Only randomized controlled trials should influence practice","All designs carry equal evidential weight","Observational designs cannot guide practice at all","Some are stronger, but all serve a purpose and can guide practice"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the distinction drawn is between designs that establish cause and effect and those that establish relationships or predictability. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which designs are observational?","choices":["Randomized controlled trials","Meta-analyses","Quasi-experimental studies","Cohort and case-control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because in both the investigator watches an exposure that occurred independently rather than allocating it. The randomized trial is the experimental example. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Colorectal cancer screening by fecal occult blood testing across a population is an example of what?","choices":["A prospective cohort study","A cross-sectional survey","A case-control study","A randomized controlled trial"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because it measures a characteristic across a population at one point in time using individual level data. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What lies at the start of the trajectory toward evidence-based information?","choices":["Ideas, from observation, biology and reasoning","Randomized controlled trials","Systematic reviews","Case reports"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they become hypotheses, then observational studies, then experimental studies, and only then evidence-based information. Imagination is named alongside them. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In which direction does a case-control study look?","choices":["Backward, from outcome to exposure","Forward, from exposure to outcome","At a single moment, with neither before the other","Forward, from an assigned intervention to outcome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: participants are sorted by whether the outcome is present, and each group is then examined for whether the risk factor was present beforehand. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
